{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/6/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"28a6681f\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nSimulate water pixels falling under gravity into basins formed by colored obstacles.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 0. Water pixels = color 1 (blue).\n- Other colors form static solid structures (walls, steps, containers).\n- Water starts in unstable positions above or beside basins.\n\nRule:\n- Each water pixel falls straight down through bg cells.\n- When blocked below, it slides diagonally through the unique open side cell.\n- When resting on a surface, it slides horizontally toward the nearest gap where it can fall again.\n- Process repeats until no water pixel moves.\n\nKey insight: Water seeks the deepest reachable basin via diagonal and horizontal sliding, not just vertical fall.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b7bacbf\" lines=\"42-56\">\n<description>\nFill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Colored lines/drawings on the grid.\n- Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.\n\nRule:\n- Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.\n- Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.\n- Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.\n\nKey insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9b30e358\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nRepeat the bottom foreground motif upward until it fills the whole grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The only foreground pixels occupy a contiguous block of rows at the bottom.\n- That bottom block is the complete vertical motif, including its background cells.\n\nRule\n- Copy the bottom motif rows repeatedly upward across the full grid.\n- Preserve horizontal alignment and vertical phase: the original first motif row remains at its input row.\n- The output shape is unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nThe blank rows above the bottom foreground block are missing repetitions of the same row motif, not empty space.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"758abdf0\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nAdvance short marker bars away from the full edge bar, turning completed two-pixel markers into edge-color marks at the opposite side.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One non-background color forms a complete one-cell-thick bar on exactly one outer edge.\n- The other foreground color appears only in the first two cells inward from that bar, lane by lane.\n\nRule\n- In each lane perpendicular to the edge bar, a one-pixel marker adjacent to the bar grows one more cell inward.\n- A two-pixel marker in those first two inward cells is cleared back to background.\n- The cleared two-pixel marker is replaced by a two-pixel mark of the edge-bar color at the opposite side of the same lane.\n\nKey insight\n- The full edge bar defines both the orientation and the direction of the local two-step marker cycle.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3bd292e8\" lines=\"13-29\">\n<description>\nRecolor wall-separated background regions alternately, starting with grey in the lower-left region.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground wall color.\n- The wall pixels form one or more 4-connected one-cell-thick segments.\n- Each wall segment separates exactly two 4-connected background regions.\nRule\n- Keep all wall pixels unchanged.\n- Label the 4-connected background regions and connect two labels when they\n  touch opposite sides of the same wall segment.\n- Color the region containing the lower-left corner grey, then flip between\n  grey and green across every wall adjacency.\nKey insight\n- The red walls define a bipartite map of regions; the lower-left background\n  region fixes which side of the alternating coloring is grey.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"97d7923e\" lines=\"81-94\">\n<description>\nRecolor vertical bars by legend-driven rank selection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Top-left: monochrome blocks touching row 0; each has color K and height k.\n- Main grid: vertical bars capped top and bottom by a single color, with solid-color interiors.\n\nRule:\n- For each legend entry (K, k), find vertical bars capped by K.\n- Rank bars by interior length (longest = rank 1).\n- Recolor the rank-k bar's interior to K.\n\nKey insight: Legend height is a rank index into length-sorted bars, not a literal size.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c9680e90\" lines=\"52-67\">\n<description>\nMove each mirrored bottom source pixel to the far end of its trail and redraw the top half as its reflection.\n\nInput structure\n- A solid non-background row separates equal-height top and bottom halves.\n- The top half has one marker color on the background.\n- In the bottom half, exactly one color has positions that mirror the top markers across the separator, and the other color forms trails touching those source pixels.\n\nRule\n- Identify the bottom source color by reflecting each bottom color into the top half and matching the top marker set.\n- For every bottom source pixel, follow its unique 4-connected trail of the other bottom color to the trail endpoint.\n- Clear the old top markers, bottom sources, and trails; place the source color at each endpoint and place the top marker color at the reflected endpoint.\n\nKey insight\n- The top half is only a mirror display of the bottom moving sources; the actual motion is encoded by the trail attached to each bottom source pixel.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"20fb2937\" lines=\"63-77\">\n<description>\nReplace each marker below the separator with the mapped top legend square centered on that marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background surrounds a top legend, one solid non-background separator row, and a lower marker field.\n- The legend contains equal-size filled square sprites, with a singleton marker below each sprite and aligned to its center column.\n- The lower field contains only background and marker colors from the legend.\n\nRule:\n- Pair legend markers to legend sprites by left-to-right position.\n- Discard the legend and separator; keep the grid width and the rows below the separator.\n- In the lower field, replace every marker pixel by a filled square of the paired sprite color, centered at the marker location.\n\nKey insight: The top area is a positional color legend, and the actual output is the post-separator marker field expanded by that legend.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f0f8a26d\" lines=\"30-44\">\n<description>\nRotate every foreground bar 90 degrees around its own center on the same canvas.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color, and all foreground pixels share one color.\nEach 4-connected foreground object is a solid one-cell-thick horizontal or vertical\nbar with odd length, with single-pixel bars allowed.\n\nRule\nClear the canvas to the background, then redraw every bar with its midpoint, length,\nand color preserved while swapping horizontal and vertical orientation.\n\nKey insight\nThe bars are independent objects: their centers and lengths identify the output\nplacement, so no matching, ordering, or global symmetry is needed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e84fef15\" lines=\"39-59\">\n<description>\nReconstruct the repeated tile from its separated copies and mark the missing pixels in the damaged copy blue.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a lattice of equal-size blocks separated by one-cell solid rows\n  and columns of the same separator color.\n- All blocks are copies of one motif except exactly one block, where some\n  motif pixels have been overwritten by the motif background color.\n- Blue is absent from the input and is reserved as the output marker color.\n\nRule:\n- Recover the motif by taking the unique majority color at each local block\n  coordinate across all block copies.\n- Find the single block that differs from this consensus motif.\n- Return the consensus motif, recoloring the differing local coordinates blue.\n\nKey insight:\n- The repeated copies provide redundancy: the damaged positions are exactly\n  the coordinates where the lone nonconforming copy has background instead of\n  the consensus motif color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4a21e3da\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nCut a sprite along border-control rays and slide pieces to grid corners.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, fg (main object, ~centered), ctrl (1-2 pixels on grid edges).\n\nRule:\n- Draw rays inward from each ctrl pixel along its row/col, painting only bg cells up to the farthest fg pixel (intermittent).\n- Rays partition the grid; fg on the cut line stays in place.\n- Cut pieces in regions touching a control edge slide to that corner; others vanish.\n\nKey insight: Only regions sharing a border edge with a control pixel keep their piece; the ray skips fg cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e8686506\" lines=\"172-185\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.\n- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.\n\nRule:\n- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.\n- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.\n- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.\n\nKey insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2d0172a1\" lines=\"209-224\">\n<description>\nConvert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
